// Stage-2 port of runtime/shims/ (#1368): the C-runtime symbols this
// project's runtime references but that neither a libc (there is none — static
// binaries with zero runtime dependency) nor compiler-rt supplies.
//
// Two symbols were in scope. `strlen` is here; `getauxval` turned out not to
// need a shim at all — see the note at the bottom of this file, which is the
// interesting part of this module.
//
// `memcpy`/`memset`/`memmove`/`__divti3` are deliberately NOT here. Hand-rolling
// `memcpy` would recurse, because a naive implementation lowers straight back
// into a `memcpy` call. Nothing needs them today: none of the four is defined or
// referenced anywhere in `libbitrt.a` on any target, so the codegen that would
// have emitted those calls does not.
//
// THIS MODULE IS CURRENTLY DEFINED BUT UNCONSUMED, and that is worth knowing
// before changing it. `libbitrt.a` is 22 `runtime_*.o` members on every target,
// and `runtime_shims.o` is the sole definer of `strlen` (`_strlen` on Darwin) in
// all three. No member carries an undefined `strlen`, so the archive member is
// never pulled: `bit-out/bin/bit` links without it. The file therefore proves the
// `@symbol` pin works and stands ready for the first real consumer; it is not
// currently load-bearing, and a change here will not be caught by a link error.
//
// It needs no OS-conditional primitive — it contains neither `syscall` nor
// `extern function` — so it COMPILES for every target, and `bit build -c` emits
// the pinned `strlen` on all three.

import { strlenScan } from "./scan"

// `@symbol` (§11.9) pins the emitted symbol to exactly `strlen`. Without it
// the definition would be emitted as `m<id>$strlen`, where `<id>` is an
// ordinal the importing build assigns, and no C-ABI caller asking for plain
// `strlen` would resolve. That pin is the whole reason this shim can exist in
// Bit at all.
//
// The scan itself lives in `./scan` — see that module for why the pin and the
// behaviour have to be separable.
//
// NOT `@nosplit`: the scan allocates nothing, so the attribute would buy
// nothing here.
export @symbol("strlen") fn strlen(s: *u8): i64 {
  return strlenScan(s)
}

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// getauxval — PORTED, and it lives in ../auxv rather than here (#1417).
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
//
// This file used to record `getauxval` as blocked. The blocker was real but has
// been resolved differently than expected, so the reasoning is worth keeping.
//
// The shim was originally specified as a PAIR: `setTable(table)` called once by
// `runtime/root/`'s `rtStartMain` with the real auxv the kernel put on the
// initial stack, and `getauxval(kind)` later scanning that table. A C-ABI caller
// invokes `getauxval` by its bare name with a single argument, so the table
// cannot be threaded through as a parameter — it has to survive between two
// unrelated calls, in process-global mutable memory, which Bit did not have.
// #1402 has since landed that storage.
//
// The port did not need it. The pair collapses to one function once the table
// pointer can be ASKED FOR rather than remembered: `auxv()` (ABI.md §19) returns
// the address the process entry captured, and ../auxv/auxv.bit does the scan.
// No module-level state, so nothing to initialize and no ordering hazard between
// a setter and its first reader.
//
// It is NOT in this file because this file's job is C-ABI symbol pins, and the
// Bit accessor is not one of those: `strlen` must be emitted under exactly that
// name for a C-ABI caller to resolve it, whereas nothing outside Bit calls the
// auxv accessor. Pinning it as `getauxval` would claim a C name for a function
// no C caller wants.
